## Appendix A Optical Theorem and Some Useful Parameters

The optical theorem introduces the relationship between the behavior of the imaginary part of the forward scattering amplitude or the scattering amplitude at \(t=0\), the scattering angle and the total cross section.

\[Imf_{el}(t=0)=\frac{p}{4\pi}\sigma_{tot} \tag{202}\]

where \(f_{el}\) is the scattering amplitude of elastic scattering, \(p\) is the magnitude of the center of mass three-momentum of the incident particle, and \(\sigma_{tot}\) is the total cross section.

The optical theorem states that the imaginary part of the forward amplitude, which is proportional to the total cross section, grows with energy, while no such constraint exists over the real part [11].

